<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="BUPu9iGdBgptEA6uMX1pIl" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="Iex1VNphuDkdrxdo9y50I4" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="C6vLVwaUApM8OHKmbfnptv" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="MDnkk4awzmeFoOObehT3sl" Location="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="RD44OFA2oRmLB1j2Ntb35C" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="CFSMLl3kGPtThMfo4yM8LF">
    <Canvas Id="CxZGeDZGXFmmRMnr3fe7Qh" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="N5kRRBuW7MFqTreBgqPsET">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="Eq8PaujhTiNClRVOQULju2">
        <Canvas Id="FNGSrATlyPw2zUIUy6DOCx" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="IUeAQNvovq9pCJjjuSRN3w" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1560" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="11 - Do you really want to ask 100,000 points one by one?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A hundred thousand points. A square that follows your mouse. The points inside the square light&#xD;&#xA;up. Two machines are answering the same question at once, and the two rows of numbers in the&#xD;&#xA;patch are their receipts:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  BRUTE FORCE    Tested 100,000       Accepted  427&#xD;&#xA;  INDEXED        Candidates   612     Accepted  427&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The Accepted numbers are equal, and they always will be - both machines ask the exact same&#xD;&#xA;question, Contains, of every point they look at. The difference is HOW MANY POINTS THEY LOOK&#xD;&#xA;AT. One asks all hundred thousand, every frame. The other asks about six hundred.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THAT IS THE WHOLE CHAPTER. A spatial index does not answer your question. It narrows who&#xD;&#xA;gets asked.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CANDIDATES IS NOT ACCEPTED. Look at the indexed row again: 612 candidates, 427 accepted. The&#xD;&#xA;index works on bounding boxes, because that is what makes it fast, and it never looks at&#xD;&#xA;the geometry itself. So it hands back everything whose box MIGHT overlap - a maybe - and&#xD;&#xA;the exact test on those few finishes the job. Here the points are points, so the gap is&#xD;&#xA;only the square's edges; with real shapes the gap is bigger, and a chapter-04 diagonal line&#xD;&#xA;is a candidate for a corner it passes nowhere near.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A CANDIDATE IS NOT A RESULT. Skip the second step and your map is wrong by exactly the&#xD;&#xA;difference between those two numbers, and nothing will tell you.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;INDEXES BUILT SAYS 1. It should stay there. Building the index over a hundred thousand&#xD;&#xA;points costs real time; doing it sixty times a second would be the same as brute force,&#xD;&#xA;wearing a hat. The points are made once, inside the CACHE region, and handed to&#xD;&#xA;SpatialIndex as the same objects frame after frame - so it builds once and then only&#xD;&#xA;answers. If that number ever climbs while you watch, something upstream is re-making its&#xD;&#xA;geometry every frame, and the index is doing nothing for you.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE ONE KNOB IS THE MOUSE. Half Size is a discovery: make the square big and both&#xD;&#xA;Accepted numbers grow together, Candidates hugging Accepted from above. Make it tiny and&#xD;&#xA;the index returns a handful while brute force still tests every single point. The brute&#xD;&#xA;force row never changes its first number. That is the point of it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Feel the frame rate. The brute-force loop is really running - a hundred thousand&#xD;&#xA;  Contains per frame - and this patch is deliberately as slow as that honestly is: about&#xD;&#xA;  8 frames a second on the machine it was built on, measured, not estimated. Disable that&#xD;&#xA;  ForEach region and feel the difference. Then re-enable it, because the comparison is the&#xD;&#xA;  lesson - and because the slow number is the one that tells you why indexes exist.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Change Count in RandomSpread to 1,000,000. Watch Indexes Built tick to 2 - once, because&#xD;&#xA;  the points changed - and then hold.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Replace the square with a Buffer around the cursor from chapter 05. Query takes any&#xD;&#xA;  geometry; it only ever reads its bounding box.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT HAPPENED?&#xD;&#xA;  Every spatial question you have asked so far - Contains in 03, Distance in 04 - was asked&#xD;&#xA;  of one geometry. Real data comes in hundreds of thousands, and asking each one is a cost&#xD;&#xA;  that grows with the data, not with the question. The index is the standard answer, and&#xD;&#xA;  the standard answer has a shape: a cheap broad phase over boxes, then the exact phase over&#xD;&#xA;  the survivors. Both phases are on this canvas, labelled.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  envelope / bounding box   the smallest axis-aligned rectangle round a geometry&#xD;&#xA;  spatial index             a structure that finds, by box, what MIGHT be somewhere&#xD;&#xA;  R-tree / STRtree          the usual shape of one: boxes inside boxes inside boxes. STR is&#xD;&#xA;                            Sort-Tile-Recursive, the way this one packs itself&#xD;&#xA;  candidate                 an item whose box passed. Not yet an answer&#xD;&#xA;  exact predicate           the real test - Contains, Intersects, Within - run on candidates&#xD;&#xA;  broad phase / narrow phase   the two steps, named the way game engines name them&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  You have met this idea wearing other clothes: broad-phase collision, a BVH in a ray&#xD;&#xA;  tracer, an octree, a KD-tree, a uniform grid for particles. Same bargain every time -&#xD;&#xA;  spend a little on a structure so you can stop asking everyone.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS PATCH USES ONE PACKAGE, VL.NetTopologySuite, and it is the first chapter to use its&#xD;&#xA;NTS.Index category - SpatialIndex and Query, which were added for this chapter and have&#xD;&#xA;never been on a screen before this one. A unit needing one package normally belongs in that&#xD;&#xA;package's own help folder; the exemption is written into this pack's rules for the spine,&#xD;&#xA;and the sequence needs the index lesson here, between one coordinate system and a field.&#xD;&#xA;No map, on purpose: a hundred thousand features on a tile layer is a different chapter's&#xD;&#xA;problem, and this one is about the question, not the picture.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 12              what if space is not made of objects at all&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 03              the exact predicate, alone, on one shape&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 09              real data, in the hundreds - now imagine it in the millions">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <!-- ===== A. the points, made ONCE ===== -->
          <Pad Id="OmsrQFRWEGCIWY43gP7UaR" Bounds="60,-60,560,30" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A HUNDRED THOUSAND POINTS, MADE ONCE. RandomSpread hands out the same spread every frame; the&#xD;&#xA;Cache region makes the NTS Points once and holds them. Same objects each frame is what lets the index stay built.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
